e at Solid or Safe D; all three prediction markets price a Democratic win near 97%", "Weiser and Colorado Democrats hold a clear fundraising edge while Marx runs an unconventional, debate-skipping campaign", "Open seat with no general-election polling keeps uncertainty wider than usual"], "rationale": "Colorado's Democratic lean and a favorable environment give Weiser a strong floor, every expert rater calls the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and markets price a Democratic win at roughly 97 percent. Marx's lone, scripted forum appearance and the Democrats' fundraising edge leave little evidence of a Republican path.", "watch": "A late wave of Marx self-funded advertising or the first public poll showing a single-digit race would be the main signals to watch.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Jared Polis (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Colorado's governorship is open as Jared Polis terms out, and Democrat Phil Weiser faces Republican Victor Marx in a state that has leaned about six points Democratic in recent elections. Every major race rater — the Cook Political Report, Inside Elections, RealClearPolitics, Fox News, Sabato's Crystal Ball, the Silver Bulletin, and Decision Desk HQ — places the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and all three prediction markets price a Democratic win at roughly 97 percent.\n\nThe campaign's final weeks have done little to change that picture. Weiser and Colorado Democrats hold a clear fundraising edge, while Marx has run an unconventional, largely self-funded campaign that has skipped most joint appearances; the nominees met only once, in a scripted forum. With no general-election polls available, uncertainty is wider than in a typical race, but the accumulated evidence points to a comfortable Democratic hold on the governor's office.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9695, "margin": 12.28}, {"dat